# CeeLing fact pack: the part of the C++ standard library the compiler admits today.
#
# MIGRATED 2026-11-02 from the hand tables in library_summary.cpp with NO change in behaviour
# (tests/facts/ checks that every answer is the same). Everything here is hand-written, so
# source=declared review=hand. The vector and string method lists were replaced on 2026-11-02 by facts generated from the
# library's own declarations, with admission computed by rule (docs/design/third-party-inference.md, TODO.md).
#
# Format (docs/internals/facts-format.md): one fact per line,
#     <kind> <subject> [flag ...] source=<declared|inferred> [review=<state>]
# Unknown kinds, unknown flags and a missing source= are load errors.
@pack std
@version 1
@origin hand-written, migrated from library_summary.cpp

# std::vector and std::string: which methods may be called is NOT listed here. It is computed from the scan of the library's own
# declarations (std-vector.clfacts, std-string.clfacts) by the rule in library_summary.cpp. Only what no signature can say is
# declared by hand:
#   * `shares-arg0`: push_back links its first argument into the container (like a field store), it does not consume it.
#   * `vector.own`: methods CeeLing itself defines, with no std counterpart (copyWithin).
# `resize`, `reserve`, `copyWithin` and `assign` are further limited to primitive elements by a language rule in
# escape_hatch_checker.cpp; that is policy, not a fact.
vector.note push_back shares-arg0 source=declared review=hand
vector.own copyWithin source=declared review=hand
# CeeLing's own whole-vector algorithms (no iterators): lowered to cling::vsort etc. Primitive elements only (language rule in escape_hatch_checker.cpp).
# None changes size() or reallocates, so none invalidates a span or an element observer.
vector.own sort source=declared review=hand
vector.own reverse source=declared review=hand
vector.own fill source=declared review=hand
vector.own indexOf source=declared review=hand
vector.own contains source=declared review=hand

# Free functions. `std::to_string`: number to text, returns a fresh string (nothing to borrow).
function std::stoi source=declared review=hand
function std::stod source=declared review=hand
function std::to_string source=declared review=hand
